  "live_records": {
    "q-64298": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64298",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{5t\\}+L\\{3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}=\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
