    "q-331799": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331799",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider a function $q(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{q(t)\\}=Q(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Q(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Q(s-9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
